00:35:04,000 --> 00:35:06,000
Mayor of Beechwood, Andrew Townsend.

789
00:35:06,000 --> 00:35:09,000
Oh, and I have some birthday shout-outs.

790
00:35:09,000 --> 00:35:13,000
Chief Security Officer for the installation, Brigadier General James Paddock.

791
00:35:13,000 --> 00:35:15,000
Head of the satellite project.

792
00:35:16,000 --> 00:35:17,000
Dr. Paul Nelson.

793
00:35:17,000 --> 00:35:18,000
This one's going out to you.

794
00:35:18,000 --> 00:35:19,000
It's Kathy's clown.

795
00:35:19,000 --> 00:35:20,000
One of the wives that makes a total of eight.

796
00:35:20,000 --> 00:35:23,000
Exactly the number of control devices you will be able to produce at this time.

797
00:35:23,000 --> 00:35:25,000
I'd like to see one of these devices.

798
00:35:25,000 --> 00:35:27,000
I presume they're electronic in function.

799
00:35:27,000 --> 00:35:31,000
Hmm, monster claws with drawn butter.

800
00:35:31,000 --> 00:35:32,000
I'm back, baby!

801
00:35:32,000 --> 00:35:41,000
Hey, hurry everybody.

802
00:35:41,000 --> 00:35:43,000
The new Lorna Luft album is released.

803
00:35:43,000 --> 00:35:45,000
It's Crazy Days at Beechwood Market.

804
00:35:45,000 --> 00:35:49,000
A century of pride and tradition.

805
00:35:49,000 --> 00:35:50,000
Downtown Winnetka.

806
00:35:50,000 --> 00:35:52,000
There's not a car running in town.

807
00:35:52,000 --> 00:35:53,000
What's going on?

808
00:35:53,000 --> 00:35:55,000
What's happening in the rest of the country, Mr. Hasselt?

809
00:35:55,000 --> 00:35:57,000
I don't know why a service broke down.

810
00:35:57,000 --> 00:35:58,000
Have you seen any?

811
00:35:58,000 --> 00:35:59,000
This town was stranded in the midst of nowhere.

812
00:35:59,000 --> 00:36:02,000
Mrs. Anderson, your husband said this thing was going to happen.

813
00:36:02,000 --> 00:36:04,000
Now ask him how well we're going to get out of it.

814
00:36:04,000 --> 00:36:07,000
I'll be sure to bring that up to him.

815
00:36:07,000 --> 00:36:08,000
Keep moving.

816
00:36:08,000 --> 00:36:09,000
Nothing to be afraid of.

817
00:36:09,000 --> 00:36:11,000
Don't do that.

818
00:36:11,000 --> 00:36:13,000
It's stopped.

819
00:36:13,000 --> 00:36:15,000
So who am I? Doogie Howser?

820
00:36:15,000 --> 00:36:16,000
Get out of here, grandma.

821
00:36:16,000 --> 00:36:20,000
The moment I met you, I swear.

822
00:36:20,000 --> 00:36:24,000
Oh yeah? Well, I saw your act last night.

823
00:36:24,000 --> 00:36:25,000
You're not funny.

824
00:36:25,000 --> 00:36:26,000
Ooh.

825
00:36:26,000 --> 00:36:30,000
Yeah, you, uh...

826
00:36:30,000 --> 00:36:32,000
I can punch you.

827
00:36:32,000 --> 00:36:34,000
Well, none of the other cars will work.

828
00:36:34,000 --> 00:36:37,000
I got a tiger in my tank, sweetie.

829
00:36:37,000 --> 00:36:38,000
New from Webble.

830
00:36:40,000 --> 00:36:43,000
How come you said Tom and Clyde live nearby?

831
00:36:43,000 --> 00:36:46,000
They do when you're driving.

832
00:36:46,000 --> 00:36:48,000
Well, he has pants.

833
00:36:48,000 --> 00:36:51,000
What is that crappy special effect?

834
00:36:51,000 --> 00:36:53,000
How do they get away with that?

835
00:36:53,000 --> 00:36:54,000
Roger Corman.

836
00:36:54,000 --> 00:36:55,000
I've got to stop to ask it.

837
00:36:55,000 --> 00:36:57,000
Ah, I've missed me.

838
00:36:57,000 --> 00:37:00,000
What an ugly creature. I've never seen anything like it.

839
00:37:00,000 --> 00:37:01,000
Neither have I.

840
00:37:01,000 --> 00:37:03,000
Must be some kind of cave bat.

841
00:37:03,000 --> 00:37:07,000
Remind me to ask Tom about it. He knows zoology.

842
00:37:07,000 --> 00:37:10,000
Well, let's go. We'll never get there standing here.

843
00:37:10,000 --> 00:37:13,000
I kid the end of the world, but I love it.

844
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:18,000
Why does his car work? Because he takes care of it and brings it in for routine maintenance work.

845
00:37:18,000 --> 00:37:21,000
And it doesn't clown around behind the wheel on A&E.

846
00:37:21,000 --> 00:37:23,000
That's my Peter Graves.

847
00:37:23,000 --> 00:37:24,000
That was pretty bad.

848
00:37:24,000 --> 00:37:25,000
What's he working on?

849
00:37:25,000 --> 00:37:26,000
Cute.

850
00:37:26,000 --> 00:37:29,000
Ah, that's all we need.

851
00:37:29,000 --> 00:37:31,000
What do you mean, de-energize?

852
00:37:31,000 --> 00:37:34,000
We stop power at source. That means electricity, steam, water,

853
00:37:34,000 --> 00:37:35,000
Even you?

854
00:37:35,000 --> 00:37:36,000
Everything.

855
00:37:36,000 --> 00:37:38,000
Water? You mean the faucets won't work?

856
00:37:38,000 --> 00:37:39,000
That's right.

857
00:37:39,000 --> 00:37:41,000
Oh, that's nonsense.

858
00:37:41,000 --> 00:37:45,000
Cut that out.

859
00:37:45,000 --> 00:37:47,000
Thought you said it wouldn't work.

860
00:37:47,000 --> 00:37:49,000
Sure, that works. It belongs to me.

861
00:37:49,000 --> 00:37:51,000
Are you in cahoots?

862
00:37:51,000 --> 00:37:54,000
Paul never used to make deals with the devil before.

863
00:37:54,000 --> 00:37:58,000
Jerk tried to kill me with a rock. I hate biography.

864
00:37:58,000 --> 00:38:03,000
Are you enjoying our little drive, buddy?

865
00:38:05,000 --> 00:38:06,000
Ow. Ow.

866
00:38:06,000 --> 00:38:12,000
I understand now. Ronald Reagan would make a great president.

867
00:38:12,000 --> 00:38:15,000
Yeah.

868
00:38:15,000 --> 00:38:17,000
Hmm.

869
00:38:17,000 --> 00:38:20,000
A flying bear claw.

870
00:38:20,000 --> 00:38:21,000
No, it's a pita pocket.

871
00:38:21,000 --> 00:38:23,000
No, it's a sealed sandwich.

872
00:38:23,000 --> 00:38:29,000
That's right, officer. Give a hoot.

873
00:38:32,000 --> 00:38:33,000
Had a laugh.

874
00:38:33,000 --> 00:38:37,000
Ah, for fun. I told you if we skipped, we'd get here faster.

875
00:38:37,000 --> 00:38:41,000
Hurry, run, honey. Out the back. Here they come.

876
00:38:41,000 --> 00:38:42,000
Come in.

877
00:38:42,000 --> 00:38:46,000
Nice to see you folks. Just get back from the Olympics.

878
00:38:46,000 --> 00:38:47,000
Oh, don't start with me.

879
00:38:47,000 --> 00:38:50,000
I broke down about 100 miles up the road.

880
00:38:50,000 --> 00:38:52,000
It shows the wrong day to go for a ride.

881
00:38:52,000 --> 00:38:53,000
Can you run me down to Phil's garage?

882
00:38:53,000 --> 00:38:54,000
Well, just run you down.

883
00:38:54,000 --> 00:38:57,000
I'm not sure that wouldn't do you any good, but I will ask you inside for a drink.

884
00:38:57,000 --> 00:38:58,000
You look all in.

885
00:38:58,000 --> 00:38:59,000
A drink I can use.

886
00:38:59,000 --> 00:39:00,000
I'll get some ice.

887
00:39:00,000 --> 00:39:02,000
Hey, your carpeting doesn't work.

888
00:39:02,000 --> 00:39:03,000
What's your pleasure?

889
00:39:03,000 --> 00:39:04,000
Model trains.

890
00:39:04,000 --> 00:39:06,000
I'll have some bourbon and soda.

891
00:39:06,000 --> 00:39:10,000
Somebody can just pour a bucket of water over my head and then shrink my feet.

892
00:39:10,000 --> 00:39:11,000
Well, just shrink your head.

893
00:39:11,000 --> 00:39:12,000
What time did your car die?

894
00:39:12,000 --> 00:39:14,000
Three past three.

895
00:39:14,000 --> 00:39:16,000
That's when all the clocks stopped.

896
00:39:16,000 --> 00:39:17,000
They're yours?

897
00:39:17,000 --> 00:39:18,000
Mm-hmm.

898
00:39:18,000 --> 00:39:19,000
Do you need anything else?

899
00:39:19,000 --> 00:39:20,000
No.

900
00:39:20,000 --> 00:39:23,000
Honey, would you get that crow out of the fridge? I'm going to make them eat it now.

901
00:39:23,000 --> 00:39:24,000
Why would you want the car died?

902
00:39:24,000 --> 00:39:26,000
Could have had a broken axle or anything.

903
00:39:26,000 --> 00:39:28,000
Could have. It didn't.

904
00:39:29,000 --> 00:39:31,000
Land down just like every other car did today.

905
00:39:31,000 --> 00:39:32,000
Yep. Did it myself.

906
00:39:32,000 --> 00:39:33,000
I like it.

907
00:39:33,000 --> 00:39:35,000
Mr. Love's company, but that's carrying it too far.

908
00:39:35,000 --> 00:39:37,000
What's going on?

909
00:39:37,000 --> 00:39:39,000
Making cocktails. What do you think?

910
00:39:39,000 --> 00:39:40,000
Scotch and water.

911
00:39:40,000 --> 00:39:41,000
Relax.

912
00:39:41,000 --> 00:39:43,000
You have plenty of time.

913
00:39:43,000 --> 00:39:48,000
Say.

914
00:39:48,000 --> 00:39:50,000
Heard anything from General Paddock?

915
00:39:50,000 --> 00:39:52,000
Not a thing. Not since he left the headquarters an hour ago.

916
00:39:52,000 --> 00:39:54,000
Hey, what's happening with Sidesack?

917
00:39:54,000 --> 00:39:56,000
The jukebox runs for the general. He walks.

918
00:39:56,000 --> 00:39:57,000
How are things going inside?

919
00:39:57,000 --> 00:39:58,000
Power's still off.

920
00:39:58,000 --> 00:40:00,000
Funny thing, the auxiliary has a hand crank.

921
00:40:00,000 --> 00:40:02,000
That doesn't even work.

922
00:40:02,000 --> 00:40:05,000
I wonder what effect all this is having on my wife's big mouth.

923
00:40:05,000 --> 00:40:06,000
Oh, that's nice.

924
00:40:06,000 --> 00:40:07,000
Let me know if you hear anything from the General.

925
00:40:07,000 --> 00:40:08,000
Yeah, sure thing.

926
00:40:08,000 --> 00:40:11,000
Make money selling seeds. I got it.

927
00:40:11,000 --> 00:40:15,000
Guess the privy doesn't work either, huh?

928
00:40:15,000 --> 00:40:17,000
I know. My office is around here somewhere.

929
00:40:27,000 --> 00:40:29,000
Hey, use the other end.

930
00:40:29,000 --> 00:40:30,000
Shoot 'em.

931
00:40:30,000 --> 00:40:31,000
Does this bug you? Does this bug you? I'm not touching you.

932
00:40:31,000 --> 00:40:32,000
Does this bug you?

933
00:40:32,000 --> 00:40:36,000
Oh, great. Now I'll have to wear a turtleneck all week.

934
00:40:36,000 --> 00:40:37,000
You're it.

935
00:40:37,000 --> 00:40:41,000
I understand now. Gallagher is funny.

936
00:40:41,000 --> 00:40:46,000
I'll just bury you for a little snack later.

937
00:40:46,000 --> 00:40:50,000
I'm sorry, Tom. I can't bring myself to believe what you're saying.

938
00:40:50,000 --> 00:40:51,000
Give me back my cocktail.

939
00:40:51,000 --> 00:40:54,000
It's nothing new. It's been years since anyone's believed me.

940
00:40:54,000 --> 00:40:56,000
Bitter o' a tad.

941
00:40:56,000 --> 00:40:57,000
All right, let's assume you're right.

942
00:40:57,000 --> 00:41:01,000
The superior intelligence has come from Venus in my satellite.

943
00:41:01,000 --> 00:41:05,000
Established residency turned off the world's power and is about to take over the world's population.

944
00:41:05,000 --> 00:41:06,000
I'll give you all that.

945
00:41:06,000 --> 00:41:08,000
Why aren't you fighting it?

946
00:41:08,000 --> 00:41:13,000
Because the superior intelligence happens to be a personal friend of mine.

947
00:41:13,000 --> 00:41:14,000
Oh, he's way new to all of a sudden.

948
00:41:14,000 --> 00:41:18,000
I believe he's here to rescue mankind, not to conquer it as you have naturally concluded.

949
00:41:18,000 --> 00:41:21,000
A personal friend of yours.

950
00:41:21,000 --> 00:41:22,000
Name-dropper.

951
00:41:22,000 --> 00:41:23,000
Real chums.

952
00:41:23,000 --> 00:41:25,000
The days when people made fun of me are over, girls.

953
00:41:25,000 --> 00:41:27,000
You will bow down before me.

954
00:41:27,000 --> 00:41:30,000
...from mankind from what? From itself.

955
00:41:30,000 --> 00:41:31,000
Oh, now he's Ayn Rand.

956
00:41:31,000 --> 00:41:33,000
I didn't know we needed rescuing.

957
00:41:33,000 --> 00:41:37,000
Remember your theory on contained de-gravitation?

958
00:41:37,000 --> 00:41:38,000
How could I forget it?

959
00:41:38,000 --> 00:41:39,000
Yeah, we were flying that night.

960
00:41:39,000 --> 00:41:41,000
You thought you could build a craft that would de-gravitate itself.

961
00:41:41,000 --> 00:41:45,000
And so escape the Earth's draw without any fuel or engine of any kind.

962
00:41:45,000 --> 00:41:46,000
Oh, you mean a balloon?

963
00:41:46,000 --> 00:41:47,000
Good idea. What happened to it?

964
00:41:47,000 --> 00:41:50,000
Washington red tape couldn't get an appropriation.

965
00:41:50,000 --> 00:41:51,000
Mm-hmm.

966
00:41:51,000 --> 00:41:53,000
So, stupidity.

967
00:41:53,000 --> 00:41:56,000
An example of how stupidity restrains man's progress.

968
00:41:56,000 --> 00:41:58,000
I've been a continual victim of it myself.

969
00:41:58,000 --> 00:41:59,000
No argument here.

970
00:41:59,000 --> 00:42:01,000
Stupidity, fear, greed.

971
00:42:01,000 --> 00:42:02,000
Bad haircuts.

972
00:42:02,000 --> 00:42:05,000
And a new arrival is going to end all of this?

973
00:42:05,000 --> 00:42:07,000
In so short a time, you hardly realize it's been done.

974
00:42:07,000 --> 00:42:09,000
It's called the New World Order, Paul.

975
00:42:09,000 --> 00:42:11,000
Can't help feeling a sense of triumph, Paul.

976
00:42:11,000 --> 00:42:12,000
I do love to gloat.

977
00:42:12,000 --> 00:42:14,000
I'm in on the beginning of ultimate freedom.

978
00:42:14,000 --> 00:42:16,000
I predicted the possibility.

979
00:42:16,000 --> 00:42:18,000
I assist the benefactor.

980
00:42:18,000 --> 00:42:19,000
How's he gonna do it?

981
00:42:19,000 --> 00:42:20,000
Amway products.

982
00:42:20,000 --> 00:42:22,000
Can't tell you yet, Paul. It's premature.

983
00:42:22,000 --> 00:42:23,000
So is I.

984
00:42:23,000 --> 00:42:25,000
But you'll be with us very soon.

985
00:42:25,000 --> 00:42:26,000
Join us.

986
00:42:26,000 --> 00:42:28,000
I don't think so, Tom.

987
00:42:28,000 --> 00:42:34,000
I'd have to take a long, hard look at anything that was gonna change the world and me so completely.

988
00:42:34,000 --> 00:42:37,000
And I've gotta tape some wraps for A&E. Shoot me a memo.

989
00:42:37,000 --> 00:42:38,000
I'll lift to the lab.

990
00:42:38,000 --> 00:42:41,000
No point in going there. It's inoperative and useless.

991
00:42:41,000 --> 00:42:42,000
Boy, are you a downer tonight.

992
00:42:42,000 --> 00:42:43,000
Let's go.

993
00:42:43,000 --> 00:42:44,000
Will you drive us home?

994
00:42:44,000 --> 00:42:46,000
No, unless you go out to the car.

995
00:42:46,000 --> 00:42:47,000
Oh, I'd love to.

996
00:42:47,000 --> 00:42:48,000
Coffee and pie?

997
00:42:48,000 --> 00:42:49,000
No.

998
00:42:49,000 --> 00:43:07,000
Mmm, mmm. You know, that pie was delicious. Did you bake it yourself?

999
00:43:07,000 --> 00:43:10,000
Oh, no. It's just a recipe my grandmother stole from the bakery.

1000
00:43:10,000 --> 00:43:12,000
Hey, want some more?

1001
00:43:12,000 --> 00:43:13,000
Only half a gallon.

1002
00:43:13,000 --> 00:43:17,000
What are you so smug about? You look like a man who just inherited Texas.

1003
00:43:17,000 --> 00:43:19,000
Well, it may not be too far off.

1004
00:43:19,000 --> 00:43:23,000
Oh, you know, this coffee tastes like it came out of an oil derrick. What, did you strain it through a mummy?

1005
00:43:23,000 --> 00:43:26,000
Yeah, the coffee tastes like mud. Roger mud.

1006
00:43:26,000 --> 00:43:28,000
No, and the coffee wasn't half as bad as the dinner.

1007
00:43:28,000 --> 00:43:30,000
Well, I only burn it when you come home drunk.

1008
00:43:30,000 --> 00:43:32,000
Yeah, so you burn it every night.

1009
00:43:32,000 --> 00:43:34,000
Oh, don't bring that up again.

1010
00:43:34,000 --> 00:43:36,000
I have to bring it up. If I hold it in, I'll die.

1011
00:43:36,000 --> 00:43:39,000
Die? That's what this coffee tastes like? Die?

1012
00:43:39,000 --> 00:43:41,000
Did you two get enough? You hardly touched your steak.

1013
00:43:41,000 --> 00:43:43,000
I didn't want to touch it. It scared me.

1014
00:43:43,000 --> 00:43:46,000
Yeah, talk about steak being tough. I thought they retired Man-O-Wart-A-Stuck.

1015
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:48,000
Oh, what would you know about being a stud?

1016
00:43:48,000 --> 00:43:50,000
Well, the meat was better if you put that fuzzy gravy on it.

1017
00:43:50,000 --> 00:43:53,000
Oh, was that gravy? I thought the dog had been sick.

1018
00:43:53,000 --> 00:43:56,000
I've never seen Spam serve so many ways, especially in the jello.

1019
00:43:56,000 --> 00:44:00,000
You know, halfway through the dinner, my filet got up and beat the hell out of my coffee,

1020
00:44:00,000 --> 00:44:02,000
and the coffee was too weak to defend itself.

1021
00:44:02,000 --> 00:44:05,000
Oh, well, it looked like you enjoyed the marinade, or were you just being a pig?

1022
00:44:05,000 --> 00:44:07,000
Hey, the only thing that's marinated around here is Tom.

1023
00:44:07,000 --> 00:44:11,000
Oh, next time, why don't you just skip dinner and go straight to passing out on the table?

1024
00:44:11,000 --> 00:44:12,000
Okay, keep it down, you two.

1025
00:44:12,000 --> 00:44:15,000
I don't think I can keep anything down if I have to keep looking at that dessert.

1026
00:44:15,000 --> 00:44:18,000
Yeah, you know, on second thought, forget about the pie. I'd just rather eat the recipe.

1027
00:44:18,000 --> 00:44:22,000
Oh, why can't we just have a meal like a family just once we've got movie supplies?

1028
00:44:22,000 --> 00:44:34,000
More coffee?

1029
00:44:34,000 --> 00:44:36,000
I've had enough.

1030
00:44:36,000 --> 00:44:40,000
He didn't panic because logic makes him think you're insane.

1031
00:44:40,000 --> 00:44:42,000
Ooh, good one.

1032
00:44:42,000 --> 00:44:45,000
I've forgiven you like a face in the news.

1033
00:44:45,000 --> 00:44:46,000
Not tonight, anyway.

1034
00:44:46,000 --> 00:44:49,000
Now the facts are in front of you. Time you stood by me.

1035
00:44:49,000 --> 00:44:53,000
I'll stand by you, Tom. Not just because I'm your wife, but...

1036
00:44:53,000 --> 00:44:55,000
Because I'm Tammy Wynette.

1037
00:44:55,000 --> 00:44:56,000
Because I love you.

1038
00:44:56,000 --> 00:44:57,000
Oh.

1039
00:44:57,000 --> 00:44:59,000
And you're my meal ticket.

1040
00:44:59,000 --> 00:45:00,000
Do it.

1041
00:45:00,000 --> 00:45:04,000
She'll die first. She's useless and outdated, and I'm going to tell on her.

1042
00:45:04,000 --> 00:45:07,000
Hi, boy, am I hammered.

1043
00:45:07,000 --> 00:45:14,000
